    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-e/q2",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q2-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A shop owner has established over a long period of time, that 12% of the people who buy crisps, prefer the “ready salted” variety. On a certain day 65 customers bought crisps. Using a distributional approximation, find the probability that more than 9 of these 65 customers bought crisps of the “ready salted” variety. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q2-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let X ∼B(65, 0.12) be the number of customers buying ready salted crisps: [B1] [A1] The binomial parameters are n = 65, p = 0.12, q = 0.88, so np = 65(0.12). The mean is 7.8 and variance is 65(0.12)(0.88) = 6.864. [A1] Use Y ∼N(7.8, 6.864) [M1] with standard √ deviation 6.864. The event X &gt; 9 is X ≥10; its boundary is halfway between 9 and 10.\" style=\"--madas-width:665.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q2-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Use a continuity correction at 9.5: 9.5 −7.8  P(X &gt; 9) ≈P(Y &gt; 9.5) = 1 −Φ √ = 1 −Φ(0.6489 . . .) ≈1 −0.7418 = 0.2582 . [M1] [A1] 6.864\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 2 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A shop owner has established over a long period of time, that 12% of the people who buy crisps, prefer the “ready salted” variety. On a certain day 65 customers bought crisps. Using a distributional approximation, find the probability that more than 9 of these 65 customers bought crisps of the “ready salted” variety. ( ) 6"
  },
