    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A test statistic has distribution ( B 30, p . ) Given that H : p = 0.4 , H : p ≠ 0.4 , 0 1 find the critical region for the test statistic at the 5% significance level. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Test H0 : p = 0.4 against H1 : p̸ = 0.4. Under H0, X ∼B(30, 0.4). [B1] Each tail must have probability at most 0.025. [A1] P(X ≤6) ≈0.01718 P(X ≤7) ≈0.04352, [M1] [A1] P(X ≥18) ≈0.02124 P(X ≥17) ≈0.04811. For the upper tail use 1 −P(X ≤17) at 18 and 1 −P(X ≤16) at 17. At 6 and 18 the probabilities are within 2.5%; including either neighbouring value would exceed it: The critical region is {0, . . . , 6} ∪{18, . . . , 30} . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 5 marks.</p>"
