    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q13-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:561.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q13-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) The speed rises linearly from 0 to 18 m s−1, stays at 18 for T seconds, then falls linearly to 0. [M1] The graph below uses the times found in the later parts: [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:665.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-e/q13-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(b) Use the gradient (0 −18)/∆t = −0.75, so deceleration lasts 18/0.75 = 24 s . [M1] [A1] (c) (i) The distance is the area under the speed–time graph: 1 2(18)(T/4) + 18T + 1 2(18)(24) = 1512. [B1] The source uses the trapezium in one calculation: 1 2[(T/4 + T + 24) + T]18 = 1512, giving 9T/4 + 24 = 168, 9T/4 = 144, 9T = 576. This gives 20.25T = 1296, [A1] hence T = 64 s . [A1] (ii) The acceleration lasts T/4 = 16 [B1] seconds, so a = 18/16 = 1.125 m s−2 . [A1] (d) The acceleration graph has height 1.125 from 0 to 16 seconds, 0 from 16 to 80 seconds, and −0.75 from 80 to 104 seconds: [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 13 is 11 marks.</p>"
